  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=3{t}^{2}-18t+27,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  The positive direction is left to right. If the particle is stationary when $t=3\\,\\text{s},$ determine the time intervals in which the particle moves to the right.\n\n$t>$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (3{t}^{2}-18t+27)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+27t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(3)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =(3)^{3}-9(3)^{2}+27(3)+C \\\\ 0 & =27-81+81+C \\\\ C & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+27t-27=(t-3)^{3}.$\n\nTo find the time interval in which the particle is moving to the *right*, we must solve the inequality $v>0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(t-3)^{3}>0.\n$$\n\nPlotting the graph of $v(t),$ we get the following picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14094087/assets/e36c5ac354bfca5db21249d75862731c028e9f771da9070aa3382c033975e412.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $v>0$ whenever $t>3.$",
